--TEST--
Every reader `Core\Request\Part` has answers `tainted string`, including the field name the spec leaves unmarked, so a plain `string` binding refuses all three — `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`, `rule:http-server/a-part-is-a-file-iff-it-carries-a-filename`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of all three readers at once: what decides the qualifier is
// that the bytes came off a connection, and nothing about which of the three
// declarations they are. No runtime assertion can show it -- a plain value is
// assignable into a `tainted` target, so a case that only bound one would pass
// over a member that had dropped the mark. The refusal is the pin, and it is
// the binding that has to fail: `string` narrows through no qualifier bit.
//
// `name` is the one worth a case of its own. `rule:http-server/a-part-is-a-file-iff-it-carries-a-filename` marks `filename` and
// `contentType` and says nothing about the field name, which is a peer's choice
// exactly as the other two are -- a client is under no obligation to send back
// the names the form declared. Left plain, it would have been the one launderer
// on the class, reachable by using a part's own name as a path or an identifier.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    string $field = $part->name();
    string $claimed = $part->filename();
    string $declared = $part->contentType();
}
// covers: Core\Request\Part::name, Core\Request\Part::filename, Core\Request\Part::contentType
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:15:21
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
